% ============================================================================
% heart-valves — each of the four heart valves and the two chambers/vessels it
% separates (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A cardiac-anatomy fact one rung up from the chambers: blood only moves the
% RIGHT way around the heart because four one-way valves sit at the doors
% between the chambers and the great vessels. A pump needs valves so the fluid
% cannot slosh back; the heart has four, and each one guards the boundary
% between exactly two places. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? valve_separates(tricuspid, $B)   % right_atrium_and_right_ventricle
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `valve_separates(valve, boundary)` carrying the table's citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% boundary WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on
% anything that is not one of the four valves.
%
% The four valves, as a little truth table (walk the blood forward through the
% heart and every door it passes is one of these):
%
%     valve       boundary it guards                    kind
%     ---------   -----------------------------------   -------------------
%     tricuspid   right_atrium_and_right_ventricle      atrioventricular
%     mitral      left_atrium_and_left_ventricle        atrioventricular
%     pulmonary   right_ventricle_and_pulmonary_trunk   semilunar
%     aortic      left_ventricle_and_aorta              semilunar
%
% The two ATRIOVENTRICULAR (cuspid) valves sit between an atrium and the
% ventricle below it — tricuspid on the right, mitral (a.k.a. bicuspid) on the
% left. The two SEMILUNAR valves sit at the base of a great vessel leaving a
% ventricle — pulmonary at the pulmonary trunk, aortic at the aorta. This is the
% concrete cardiac rung a medicine agent reasons UP from: before it can reason
% about a murmur, a regurgitant valve, or a stenosis, it must first know which
% two places each valve stands between, the way a first-year student does. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind the boundary and recall the valve that guards
% it:
%
%     ? valve_separates($V, left_ventricle_and_aorta)   % aortic
%
% Something that is not one of the four valves — the eustachian valve, the
% coronary sinus, a chamber — has no row, so a recall on it ABSTAINS rather than
% inventing a boundary. That honest-abstention property is what makes the table
% safe to reason from.
%
% Each `boundary` value is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be a faithful
% short snake_case paraphrase of the TWO places the source names for that
% valve — never a boundary the source does not state. Note the source's own
% wording is preserved: the pulmonary valve's downstream vessel is the
% "pulmonary trunk" (not "pulmonary artery"), so the atom reads
% ...and_pulmonary_trunk.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every valve→boundary pair
% was read out of a fetched U.S. National Cancer Institute page, and any valve
% that could not be grounded there would have been dropped). All four spans come
% from the SAME primary U.S. government reference, the NCI SEER Training Modules
% "Structure of the Heart" (training.seer.cancer.gov), each quoted here
% character-for-character so any reader can re-check it. The two
% atrioventricular boundaries are fixed by chaining the defining sentence with
% the naming sentence:
%
%   (defining) "The valves between the atria and ventricles are called
%    atrioventricular valves (also called cuspid valves)."
%
%   tricuspid → right_atrium_and_right_ventricle
%     "The right atrioventricular valve is the tricuspid valve."
%     (the RIGHT atrioventricular valve ⇒ between the right atrium and the
%      right ventricle, per the defining sentence above)
%
%   mitral → left_atrium_and_left_ventricle
%     "The left atrioventricular valve is the bicuspid, or mitral, valve."
%     (the LEFT atrioventricular valve ⇒ between the left atrium and the left
%      ventricle, per the defining sentence above)
%
%   pulmonary → right_ventricle_and_pulmonary_trunk
%     "The valve between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk is the
%      pulmonary semilunar valve."
%
%   aortic → left_ventricle_and_aorta
%     "The valve between the left ventricle and the aorta is the aortic
%      semilunar valve."
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single strongest span: the SEER sentence that names the
% first row's valve (tricuspid → the right atrioventricular valve). Every OTHER
% row's per-fact source and verbatim span is listed above, so each boundary
% remains independently checkable. The source is a primary U.S. government NCI
% reference (training.seer.cancer.gov), so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table valve_separates {
    columns valve, boundary

    row (tricuspid, right_atrium_and_right_ventricle)   % "The right atrioventricular valve is the tricuspid valve." + AV = between atria and ventricles
    row (mitral, left_atrium_and_left_ventricle)        % "The left atrioventricular valve is the bicuspid, or mitral, valve." + AV = between atria and ventricles
    row (pulmonary, right_ventricle_and_pulmonary_trunk) % "The valve between the right ventricle and pulmonary trunk is the pulmonary semilunar valve."
    row (aortic, left_ventricle_and_aorta)              % "The valve between the left ventricle and the aorta is the aortic semilunar valve."

    source "The right atrioventricular valve is the tricuspid valve."
    locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/cardiovascular/heart/structure.html"
    trust authoritative
}
